    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d493">

                <pb facs="0511" n="427" xml:id="pg427"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. No. 763.72112/5741</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Consul General at Saloniki</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Horton</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1917-12-06">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">December 6, 1917</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Your November 22. War Trade Board advises your question is covered by fact that
                    Trading with Enemy Act applies to allies of enemy and that liability American
                    firms in regard existing contracts would be determined by Greek laws but their
                    liability cannot affect policy of War Trade Board.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lansing</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
